public & private IP addressing in Azure Deploy Azure VPN Gateway

Part A — Theory 

1) Public vs Private IP in Azure
•	Private IP: lives inside a VNet/subnet (RFC1918). Used for VM-to-VM and internal traffic.
o	Assignment: Dynamic (default) or Static (pin it) on the NIC’s IP configuration.
o	Scope: VNet only (reachable over peering/VPN).
•	Public IP (PIP): routable from the Internet; attaches to a NIC, Load Balancer, App Gateway, VPN Gateway, etc.
o	SKU: Standard (secure by default; closed until you allow via NSG) vs Basic (legacy).
o	Allocation: Static (IP won’t change) or Dynamic (may change).
•	Common patterns
o	Prod VMs: no public IP, reached via Bastion / VPN / LB.
o	Internet-facing apps: PIP on Load Balancer / App Gateway, not on the VM.
•	Name resolution: optional DNS label on PIP → myapp.region.cloudapp.azure.com.

2) Where NSGs fit
•	NSG = stateful firewall at subnet and/or NIC.
•	Rules: priority (100–4096), source/destination, port, protocol, service tags (e.g., Internet, Virtual Network, AzureLoadBalancer).
•	Defaults: allow intra-VNet, deny all inbound from Internet. Your lower-number (higher priority) rules override.

3) VPN Gateway (what & why)
•	Managed VPN appliance Microsoft runs for you.
•	Lets your PC (Point-to-Site) or on-premises network (Site-to-Site) reach your VNet’s private IPs.
•	Route-based (modern, needed for most scenarios).
•	Works with IKEv2 and OpenVPN (TLS).
•	You’ll do Point-to-Site (P2S) today (your Windows laptop ↔ VNet).
