Part C — Hands-on Lab 2: Point-to-Site VPN Gateway (Windows laptop ↔ VNet)

What you’ll build
Your Windows PC connects via Azure VPN to the VNet; then you’ll RDP to vm-web-01 using its private IP.
Tip: VPN Gateway creation is a managed deployment; just configure and let Azure build it.

Step 1 — Prepare the VNet for a gateway
1.	GatewaySubnet (required name)
•	In vnet-ip-lab, add subnet GatewaySubnet with address 10.20.255.0/27 (or any free /27).
The name must be GatewaySubnet.

Step 2 — Public IP for the gateway
•	Create PIP: pip-vpngw (SKU Standard, Static).

Step 3 — Create the VPN Gateway
•	Create Virtual network gateway:
o	Name:  vpngw-ip-lab
o	Region: same as VNet
o	Gateway type: VPN
o	VPN type: Route-based
o	SKU: VpnGw1 (good for lab)
o	Virtual network: vnet-ip-lab
o	GatewaySubnet: select the one you made
o	Public IP: pip-vpngw
•	Create (Azure provisions it for you in the background).
•	While doing this I made mistake to make subnet for this 
•	Coz Azure is case sensitive 


✅ Fix Steps
1.	Go to your Virtual Network → vnet-ip-lab.
2.	Under Subnets, click + Subnet.
3.	Enter the Name exactly as GatewaySubnet (Azure is case-sensitive for this).
4.	Assign a subnet range (example: 10.0.255.0/27 if your VNet is 10.0.0.0/16).
o	It must not overlap with other subnets.
o	Keep it small (at least /27).
5.	Save.
6.	Now redeploy your Virtual Network Gateway (vpngw-ip-lab) and select:
o	Virtual network = vnet-ip-lab
o	Subnet = GatewaySubnet
o	Public IP = pip-vpngw (or the new one you created)

•	Quick Check: Did you maybe name your subnet Gateway-subnet or gw-subnet instead of GatewaySubnet? That’s a very common mistake.

Step 4 — Configure Point-to-Site (P2S) on the gateway
 
1.	Open the gateway → Point-to-site configuration → Configure now.
2.	Address pool for VPN clients: 172.16.0.0/24 (must NOT overlap with your home LAN or VNet).
3.	Tunnel type: OpenVPN and IKEv2 (both).
 
4.	Authentication: Choose Azure certificate (simple for labs).
Generate certificates on your Windows PC (PowerShell)
This creates a root cert (uploaded to Azure) and a client cert (installed on your PC).

Open PowerShell (Run as Administrator) on your laptop and run:
# 1) Create Root Certificate
$rootCert = New-SelfSignedCertificate `
  -Type Custom -KeySpec Signature `
  -Subject "CN=AdityaP2SRootCert" `
  -KeyExportPolicy Exportable `
  -HashAlgorithm sha256 -KeyLength 2048 `
  -CertStoreLocation "Cert:\CurrentUser\My" `
  -KeyUsageProperty Sign -KeyUsage CertSign

# Export the root cert (CER, public only) to upload to Azure
Export-Certificate -Cert $rootCert -FilePath "$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\P2SRootCert.cer"

# 2) Create a Client Certificate signed by the root
$clientCert = New-SelfSignedCertificate `
  -Type Custom -KeySpec Signature `
  -Subject "CN=AdityaP2SClientCert" `
  -KeyExportPolicy Exportable `
  -HashAlgorithm sha256 -KeyLength 2048 `
  -CertStoreLocation "Cert:\CurrentUser\My" `
  -Signer $rootCert `
  -TextExtension @("2.5.29.37={text}1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.2")  # Client Authentication

5.	Upload root cert to Azure
•	In the P2S config → Upload the file P2SRootCert.cer.
•	Give it a friendly name, e.g., RootCert-Aditya.
6.	Install the client cert on your PC
•	Export the client cert as PFX (with password), then import to Current User store:
# Export client cert as PFX (you’ll be prompted for a password)
Export-PfxCertificate -Cert $clientCert `
  -FilePath "$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop\P2SClientCert.pfx" `
  -Password (Read-Host -AsSecureString "Enter PFX password")
 
•	Double-click the .pfx, import to Current User (Personal store). Remember the password you set.
7.	Save P2S configuration on the gateway.

If It Doesn’t create then make sure you have extra space to create public IP
Step 5 — Install Azure VPN Client & add the profile
1.	Install Azure VPN Client from Microsoft Store.
2.	On the gateway → Point-to-site configuration → Download VPN client (OpenVPN format).
3.	Open Azure VPN Client → Import the downloaded profile (it’s a zip; pick the xml/ovpn inside).
4.	Click Connect in Azure VPN Client.
 
Step 6 — RDP to your VM over private IP
1.	Get vm-web-01 private IP (e.g., 10.20.1.4).
2.	Ensure VM’s NSG allows RDP 3389 from Virtual Network (not only “My IP”).
o	Inbound rule example:
	Priority 100, Source Virtual Network, Dest Any, Port 3389, Protocol TCP, Action Allow.
o	 
3.	Use Remote Desktop to 10.20.1.4.
o	You should connect successfully without any public IP on the VM.
